.c
Pseudo-Steve
.c
By
.c
Stephen Willson
.blank 2
.c
ICS 199 SQ 80 (Granger)
.sp 2
.ap
.blank 3
This paper
addresses the issue of "intelligently" searching a knowledge tree.
Given a history of the search patterns specified for a tree, it uses this
history to infer certain intentions and/or possible errors on the part
of the searcher.
        The first appendix contains a listing of the tree
used by Pseudo-Steve to track down information.  The search begins at the
top-most node and then proceeds down the tree as certain keywords are
encountered.  The keywords are arranged as hierarchically as my mind could
arrange them, with more general categories near the top of the tree and
information more specific to each category stored in the lower branches of
the tree.  If the search terminates at a leaf, the search is considered
a "hit", but if the search terminates at some higher level of the tree,
then it is considered a "miss".
        By using historical information about "hits" and "misses", and
also a trace of the last path down the tree, the program attempts to
infer the missing information not supplied by the user.  Below, the
second question is answered by assuming the user meant "Steves office hours"
because not enough information was contained in the question to make a
"hit".
.sp 1.b
.lit
        Yes?  When are Kens office hours
          Ken's office hours are Tuesday and Thursday from 1:30-2:00
        Yes?  What about Steves
          Steve's office hours are Tuesday and Thursday from 11:00-12:00
.el
.sp 2.b
        The program also looks at patterns of hits and misses to (hopefully)
give the user a hint on how to instruct the program to continue the search.
Below, the program guesses that the program must have mis-interpreted a
question because the last two questions were hits to the same answer.
.sp 1.b
.lit
        Yes?  What is assignment four
          Assignment four is the compiler
        Yes?  When is it due
          Assignment four is due June 7 at the beginning of class
        Yes?  When is the compiler due
          Either I just responded to that,
          or we aren't communicating.
          Perhaps you should try using different words
          that have meanings I can separate easier.
.el
.sp 2.b
        Note:  the program assumes a co-operative user.
        The following section contains the five rules the program
uses to determine the type of response that is appropriate.  Each
rule assumes the previous rules have been checked and found not
to be true.
.b.c
Pseudo-Steve's Inference Rules
.lm+10
.i-5
Rule 1: If the answer to the previous two questions was exactly the same,
and the answer was a "hit" both times,
then infer that the user meant something different in one of the questions.
.b
.i-5
Response: Either I just responded to that or
we're not communicating here.
Perhaps you should try using different words
that have meanings I can separate easier.
.b
.i-5
Rule 2: If the answer to the previous two questions was exactly the same,
and the answer was a "miss" both times,
then infer that the user is leaving out some critical (high level)
piece of searching information.
.b
.i-5
Response: I think you are leaving out some contextual information
that I should know in order to figure out what you mean.
Try giving me more context.
.b
.i-5
Rule 3: If the most recent answer was a "miss", then
infer that the user wants more information to guide his search.
.b.i-5
Response: Maybe it will help if I tell you that
<information from node where search terminated>
.b
.i-5
Rule 4: If the most recent answer was a hit, then
infer that this is the correct answer to his question.
.b.i-5
Response:  <information from node where search terminated>
.b
.i-5
Rule 5: If none of the other rules work, then infer that the
question is very "outside" the domain, or Pseudo-Steve doesn't
know enough words.
.b
.i-5
Response:  I can't figure out the answer, sorry, next question!
.lm-10
.b.c
Annotated Run of Pseudo-Steve
        I was a TA for the compiler construction lab course,
so that was the problem domain I chose to encode into the system.
Most of the information came from the syllabus.  The text after the
tilda ("~") is a comment which mentions which rule was used.
.b.sp 1
.lit
 
Hello, I'm Pseudo-Steve.  Type HELP for help.
 
Yes? when is the compiler due
 The compiler is due June 5 at the beginning of class.
 
Yes? when is assignment four due                ~ The compiler assignment and
  Either I just responded to that or            ~ assignment four are
  we're not communicating here.                 ~ the same assignment
  Perhaps you should try using different words
  that have meanings I can separate easier.
 
Yes? when is assignment three due               ~ Simple question
 Assignment three will be due May 8th at the beginning of class.
 
Yes? how about assignment two                   ~ Uses context information
 Assignment two will be due April 24th at the beginning of class.
 
Yes? how about assignment two available date    ~ Uses context information
 Assignment two will be available April 10th.
 
Yes? due                                        ~ Uses context information
 Assignment two will be due April 24th at the beginning of class.
 
Yes? due                                        ~ Indicates confusion about
  Either I just responded to that or            ~ question (Rule 1)
  we're not communicating here.
  Perhaps you should try using different words
  that have meanings I can separate easier.
 
Yes? assignment two                             ~ Still traces a path to
  Either I just responded to that or            ~ the same answer
  we're not communicating here.
  Perhaps you should try using different words
  that have meanings I can separate easier.
 
Yes? assignment three                           ~ Uses context information
 Assignment three will be due May 8th at the beginning of class.
 
Yes? what is it                                 ~ Uses context information
 Assignment three is a design specification for the compiler.
 
Yes? what is assignment one
 Assignment one is a lexical analyzer.
 
Yes? when                                       ~ Refers to assignment one,
  Maybe it will help if I tell you that         ~ but doesn't specify enough
 I only know when you get it and when its due.  ~ information to get to a leaf
 
Yes? when is it due                             ~ The information is supplied
 Assignment one will be due April 15th at the beginning of class.
 
Yes? how much is it worth                       ~ More context information
 Assignment one is worth ten percent of your grade.
 
Yes? when do we get it back                     ~ Again
 I don't know when or where the homework will be handed back.
 
Yes? class meets when                           ~ A "hit"
 The class meets Tuesdays and Thursdays from 2 to 3:30 pm.
 
Yes? where                                      ~ Another "hit"
 The class meets in room CS 193.
 
Yes? why                                        ~ Can't answer this, so
  Maybe it will help if I tell you that         ~ gives the user more
 The class meets in room CS 193.                ~ information
 
Yes? grunthead                                  ~ Another match
 Sensors detect an unpleasant user, please behave.
 
Yes? idiot                                      ~ "idiot" is a synonym
  Either I just responded to that or
  we're not communicating here.
  Perhaps you should try using different words
  that have meanings I can separate easier.
 
Yes? who is ken                                 ~ Doesn't know much about
  Maybe it will help if I tell you that         ~ Ken, but gives hints on
 I only know about his office and the phone number.     ~ what it does know
 
Yes? what is it                                 ~ Can't decide between
  I think you are leaving out some contextual information       ~ office and
  that I should know in order to figure out what you mean.      ~ phone number
  Try giving me more context.
 
Yes? what is the phone number                   ~ User supplies the information
 Ken's extension is (714) 555-6480.
 
Yes? where is his office                        ~ Still refers to Ken
 Ken's office is in room CS 245.
 
Yes? thank you                                  ~ Trapped by program to
 You're welcome!                                ~ indicate end of session
.el
.sp 2.blank 2
.c
Issues not Addressed by Pseudo-Steve
 
        The main feature lacking from Pseudo-Steve is some mechanism
to look at the input question and determine if there are any important
words still remaining in the question that haven't been used to guide
the search, even though the search ends at a leaf.  For example,
.b.sp 1
.lit
        Yes?  Whats the due date of assignment four
.el
.sp 2.blank 2
will answer with
.b.sp 1
.lit
          Assignment four is the compiler
.el
.blank 2.sp 2
because the keyword "what" ("whats") appears in the list of keywords
before "due".  If the program could inspect the input sentence and
determine that there is ^&important\& unused information, then it could
use that information to guide the search, and the ordering of information
in each node of the tree would be less important.
        I make no claims that Pseudo-Steve solves very many AI problems.
I think it is, however, an interesting implementation of a theoretical
idea that could be expanded along more useful avenues.  Tree searches are
done frequently every day by many systems, and to be able to have the
system infer intelligently about how the search could proceed could be
of real convenience to data-base users everywhere.
.c
Appendix
.c
The Pseudo-Steve Knowledge Tree
        The tree consists of a bunch of atoms whose values are
names of other atoms (which contain subtrees) and keywords that
indicate that the search should continue down that subtree if the
keyword is in the input text.  After a keyword is matched, it is
removed from the input text.  "NIL" as an atom name indicates a leaf
of the subtree has been reached, and the searching ends.
The question, "When are Steves office hours", causes the following
trace: [Top, Steve-stuff,
Steves-office, Steves-office-hours, Steves-office-hours-time].
.sp 1
.lit
(DEFV TOP
 ((SIMULA-STUFF SIMULA PASCAL LANGUAGE LANGUAGES)
  (THE-COMPILER COMPILER PARSER PARSING)
  (STEVE-STUFF STEVE STEVES YOUR YOU WILLSON WILLSONS)
  (MONEY-STUFF MONEY DOLLARS CASH)
  (EXAM-STUFF EXAM QUIZ TEST MIDTERM FINAL)
  (KEN-STUFF KEN KENS HERTZLER HERTZLERS TEACHERS INSTRUCTORS)
  (CLASS-STUFF CLASS)
  (ASSIGNMENT-STUFF ASSIGNMENT ASSIGNMENTS HOMEWORK HOMEWORKS)
  (MAKE-AN-APPOINTMENT APPOINTMENT SEE TALK)
  (DIS-SECTION DISCUSSION LABS DISCUSSIONS LAB)
  (SNOTTY-REPLY-TEXT BYE)
  (MACH-REPLY-TEXT QUIT EXIT Q E)
  (MINI-145-STUFF MINI-145 MINI)
  (LIFE-STUFF LIFE LIVING BEING EXISTING)
  (BAD-WORDS IDIOT MORON TURKEY GRUNTHEAD)
  (NIL "I only know about ICS 145 SQ 80 (the afternoon class)")))
 
